{"273134":{"today":"Home Brewery Building","h":10,"osm":"way/299471450","kind":"office","listEntry":1237602,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1237602","qid":"Q26530729","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Home%20Brewery%20buildings%2C%20Daybrook%20-%20geograph.org.uk%20-%2018594.jpg","architects":[["T Cecil Howitt","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Cecil_Howitt"]],"stories":[{"layer":"arnold","text":"John Robinson founded the Home Brewery in 1875; its beer mats carried a Robin Hood badge. The family sold it in 1986, and its closure cost some 400 jobs. Its building of 1936 is now Sir John Robinson House, and listed Grade II.","source":{"title":"Arnold, Nottinghamshire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arnold,_Nottinghamshire","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1875","whenSrc":{"title":"Arnold, Nottinghamshire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arnold,_Nottinghamshire","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Home Brewery"},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect-osm","who":"T Cecil Howitt","source":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","url":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/299471450","kind":"OpenStreetMap"}}],"sum":"Home Ales Brewery Office, on Sir John Robinson Way. Today it houses Home Brewery Building, an office. It is Grade II listed and was designed by T Cecil Howitt.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Gedling Borough Council","xy":[-1.13754,52.99885]},"273206":{"y":1866,"f":"exact","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"1866","h":10,"osm":"way/200941630","listEntry":1227493,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1227493","qid":"Q26521399","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Arnot%20Hill%20Park%20Lake%205748.JPG","stories":[{"layer":"arnold","text":"The mill owner John Hawksley built a large cotton mill here, working before the end of 1791 with a 60-horsepower engine, and laid out Arnot Hill Park in about 1792 as a setting for his house. His son Thomas Hawksley, born at Arnot Hill House in 1807, became the engineer who gave Nottingham clean water. The house is listed Grade II.","source":{"title":"Arnold, Nottinghamshire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arnold,_Nottinghamshire","kind":"Wikipedia"},"weblinks":[["Thomas Hawksley","note:Thomas Hawksley"]],"links":["Thomas Hawksley"],"when":"1791","whenSrc":{"title":"Arnold, Nottinghamshire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arnold,_Nottinghamshire","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Arnot Hill House","see":[["Arkwright's first mill","p693"]]},{"layer":"arnold","text":"The water engineer Thomas Hawksley was born at Arnot Hill House in 1807. His Trent Bridge waterworks, completed in 1831, gave Britain its first high-pressure constant supply, which kept dirty water out of the mains.","source":{"title":"Thomas Hawksley","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Hawksley","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Thomas Hawksley","see":[["Papplewick Pumping Station","280504"]]}],"sum":"The building was built in 1866, according to Historic England (via Wikipedia). It is Grade II listed.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Gedling Borough Council","xy":[-1.13214,52.99696]},"275384":{"today":"Arnold Labour Club","y":1720,"f":"century","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"Early 18th century","h":10,"osm":"way/354382265","listEntry":1227483,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1227483","qid":"Q26521389","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bonington%20House%20-%20geograph.org.uk%20-%201557733.jpg","stories":[{"layer":"people","text":"A plaque on this house, 79 High Street in Arnold, says that the artist Richard Parkes Bonington was born here on 26 October 1802.","source":{"title":"Richard Parkes Bonington","url":"https://openplaques.org/people/24472","kind":"Open Plaques","read":"2026-11-14"},"who":"Richard Parkes Bonington","own":1}],"sum":"Bonington House, on High Street. Today it houses Arnold Labour Club. The building dates from the 18th century, according to Historic England (via Wikipedia). It is Grade II listed.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Gedling Borough Council","xy":[-1.12953,53.00438]},"278518":{"aka":["Church of St Mary","St. Mary's Church, Arnold"],"y":1150,"f":"century","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"12th century","h":16,"osm":"way/355711762","kind":"church","listEntry":1235987,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1235987","qid":"Q7590148","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/St%20Mary%27s%20Church%2C%20Arnold%20-%20geograph.org.uk%20-%201042532.jpg","archive":[{"file":"St Marys, Arnold. October 1959 - geograph.org.uk - 57987.jpg","year":1959,"author":"Tom Courtney","licence":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Mary%27s_Church,_Arnold","cat":"St Mary's Church, Arnold","stories":[{"layer":"arnold","text":"Arnold's parish church of St Mary is believed to date from 1176. It is listed Grade II*.","source":{"title":"Arnold, Nottinghamshire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arnold,_Nottinghamshire","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1176","whenSrc":{"title":"Arnold, Nottinghamshire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arnold,_Nottinghamshire","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"St Mary's, Arnold"}],"sum":"St. Mary's Church is a parish church in Arnold, Nottinghamshire, England. Though there is no official founding date, it is estimated that the church dates back to 1176 and written records commenced in 1544.","sumSrc":{"title":"St Mary's Church, Arnold","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Mary%27s_Church%2C_Arnold"},"open":[{"what":"place of worship","web":"https://www.stmarysarnold.org.uk/"}],"council":"Gedling Borough Council","xy":[-1.12688,53.0094]}}